Hi Claudio,

I would suggest using the first approach you outline there. Sites without any species observations will still serve to provide information about the factors driving species and community-level occurrence patterns. If there is a consistent pattern in where locations without any species observations occur, that could be reflected in either (a) covariates in the model or (b) the latent (spatial) factors. You could visualize the latent factors in maps which could help to reveal some interesting community level patterns (see the appendix and github repository in this publication <https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ecy.4137> for some examples of how to do this). Including a "dummy species" in the way you outlined would lead to bias in the community-level estimates and as you point out would make the species correlations and latent factors much more difficult to interpret.

Jeff

