Hi Oscar,

You are correct that spOccupancy does not directly support including species-level covariates in the multi-species model functions (including tMsPGOcc()). However, the alternative you propose there is a completely valid option, and the function postHocLM() <https://doserlab.com/files/spoccupancy-web/reference/posthoclm> in spOccupancy is designed exactly for that purpose. You could take the species-specific posterior samples from tMsPGOcc() and use those as the response in a subsequent linear model, testing the impact of species-traits on those species-specific effects. postHocLM() allows you to take the full posterior distribution of those effects and propagate that uncertainty into the subsequent regression. This is perhaps a bit less elegant than directly allowing for species-level effects when fitting the model, but it gets the job done.

Kind regards,

Jeff

